description:
Hey @damontighe, you that night-stalking tiger beetle infestation you don't have? Well, I think I've found a solution.

@dkavanaugh, these are those infected Omus larvae I was describing to you today.

Found  3 within a meter, all dead, hanging out of their burrows during the day time, thoroughly infected with a fungus.

Originally posted to Mushroom Observer on Apr. 21, 2019.

&#8212;MO custom fields&#8212;

Comments: ????

Assuming it’s a lichen. Notice the light sky blue spots surround by solid black which seem to be surrounded by tiny white specks. Photo taken with a 15x smartphone macro lens zoomed in tight. 
  This is one of the dark discolorations on a concrete overpass. Stilesboro road over Alatoona Creek. So tiny that I could not see anything but the discoloration splotch and took a photo anyway. Very surprised to see the blue and details once home. I will return with measuring device. Hopefully better photos as well. 
@mhodges1957 I haven’t a clue on this one. Can you point me in a direction?

@chipmonk87 I can tell you exactly where I found it if you'd like to check it out. Not too far from the capital city- on sunrise carriage trail, if you get on the trail, walk straight until you see the first curve right. Instead of taking the curve, keep walking straight, into the woods... you will find a ravine of sorts with water running down it. Follow that up 150ft. It was a little up the hill from that. 

It was growing nearby umbrella magnolia, yellow buckeye, tulip tree, etc. I was vacationing there so I don't remember the details nor am I very familiar with the local flora. It wasn't after a rain or anything. Didn't look like a saprotroph as it wasn't growing on anything dead in particular. I was assuming mycorrhizal. (I don't know much about mycology)

Updated with more pictures

On Ambrosia monogyra. 
@fungal_escapades
@andrewcannon
